---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { GITHUB_URL, LICENSE_URL } from '../../data/site';

const entry = page('/docs/open-source/');
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <p>
    Hozz is <a href={LICENSE_URL}>licensed under GPL-3.0 with an App Store distribution exception</a
    >{' '}and developed in public. You can inspect the code that reads Health, stores credentials,
    writes logs and sends data.
  </p>

  <h2>What the source lets you check</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The current app has no account system.</strong> There is no service to sign in to.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>There is no hosted relay or default destination.</strong> Nothing leaves until you add
      a destination and confirm it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>There are no analytics, ads, or remote crash reports.</strong> Diagnostics stay on the
      device and redact sample values, credentials and destination secrets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Destination credentials stay in the device Keychain.</strong> Networking and storage
      code are visible in <a href={GITHUB_URL}>the repository</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why that matters</h2>

  <p>
    The central privacy claim is negative: Hozz does not send your data anywhere you did not choose.
    Using the app cannot prove an absence. Reading the networking code and its tests can.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href={GITHUB_URL}>Every line is readable</a>, including the networking and the parts that
      touch the Keychain.
    </li>
    <li>
      Privacy behaviours have tests, rather than being assertions on a page. Some of them exist
      specifically to fail if a Health value ever reached a log.
    </li>
    <li>
      This documentation is written against that source, and its factual lists are checked against
      the app's own enums rather than copied by hand.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where the guarantee ends</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz does not send your data anywhere you did not configure. It cannot make any promise about
    what happens after that.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you point Hozz at an endpoint on the public internet, the data is on that endpoint. If you
    connect the <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP server</a> to a cloud-hosted assistant, that assistant may
    upload whatever it reads. Choosing destinations you control is the part Hozz cannot do for you.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> sets out exactly what leaves the device, when, and to where.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
